# Mapbox public access token.
#
# Optional in Phase 1: without it the map renders an explanatory placeholder and
# every other part of the site works normally (D11).
#
# This value is exposed in the browser by design — that is what NEXT_PUBLIC_
# means and there is no way around it for a client-side map. URL restriction on
# the token is therefore the only real control. See docs/SECURITY.md.
#
#   1. https://account.mapbox.com/access-tokens/ → create a public token (pk.*)
#   2. Restrict it to your domains (and http://localhost:3000 for development)
#   3. Mapbox has NO hard spend cap — check the dashboard weekly. See R3.
NEXT_PUBLIC_MAPBOX_TOKEN=

# Google Places — IMPORT ONLY, never used at runtime (D36).
#
# `tools/import-places.mjs` reads this from the environment when you run it by
# hand. It is NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_, is not read by the app, and must never end up in
# a client bundle or a build.
#
#   1. https://console.cloud.google.com → enable "Places API (New)"
#   2. Create a key, and RESTRICT IT to the Places API on the same day
#   3. Set a per-API daily quota — Google Cloud quotas 429 past the limit,
#      which is a true hard stop rather than a bill (see docs/SECURITY.md)
#
#   GOOGLE_PLACES_KEY=... node tools/import-places.mjs bkk1 bar
#
# The output is a draft. Hours are FETCHED, not checked — that distinction is
# what `hoursSource: "imported"` records, and R1 is about exactly it.
GOOGLE_PLACES_KEY=

# ─── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The vote store, and the ONLY server-side thing in this project (D30, D35).
# A URL is a one-way channel: an organiser can send five friends a ballot, but
# those five have no way to send votes back to a shared place. Without this,
# several people cannot vote on the same ballot at all.
#
# What it holds: a `votes` table, nothing else. No accounts, no user table, no
# profile data. The room id IS the secret — 128 bits, unguessable — which is
# the same model the shared-day links already use. Rows expire after 24 hours,
# which is what makes "v1 has no history" architectural rather than a policy
# somebody has to remember.
#
#   1. https://supabase.com → new project (Singapore is the closest region)
#   2. Project Settings → API for all three values below
#   3. SPEND CAP ON THE SAME DAY. The free plan throttles rather than bills, so
#      free is genuinely safe; on Pro, Spend Cap defaults ON — leave it.
#   4. Free projects pause after a stretch of inactivity. Weekly use is fine; a
#      longer gap means a cold start on the exact evening someone needs it.
#      Check the current threshold rather than assuming.

NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The anon key above is exposed in the browser by design — that is what
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ means. It is safe here only because the read path uses Realtime
# Broadcast on a channel named by the unguessable room id, so the anon key is
# never used to read the table directly.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SERVICE KEY. Server-side only. Never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never in a component.
#
# It bypasses every row-level policy. A build that ships this is a total
# compromise of the project, which is why acceptance criterion 13 in
# specs/3-friends/spec.md verifies its absence by grepping the build output
# rather than by anyone inspecting the code.
#
# It is used in exactly one place: the route that validates a room id and
# inserts a vote.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
